{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-39349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-39349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "04b92a1cec458cc88a24ef43c1fd8ceda302401a697fde2f9f475d70d295f959"} {"question" "q-39349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-39349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-39349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "04b92a1cec458cc88a24ef43c1fd8ceda302401a697fde2f9f475d70d295f959"} {"question" "q-104626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "146"] ["math" "171"] ["math" "198"] ["math" "213"] ["math" "65"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "171"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "04b92a1cec458cc88a24ef43c1fd8ceda302401a697fde2f9f475d70d295f959"} {"question" "q-104764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3(e+3)"] ["math" "3({e}^{2}+9)"] ["math" "3{e}^{2}-1"] ["math" "9(3e-1)"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+27"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-104764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3({e}^{2}+9)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124028" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124028" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124028" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "04b92a1cec458cc88a24ef43c1fd8ceda302401a697fde2f9f475d70d295f959"} {"question" "q-124028" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124028" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{3}-1"] ["math" "2{e}^{6}"] ["math" "4{e}^{3}"] ["math" "{e}^{3}-4"] ["math" "{e}^{6}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124028" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff6694a"} {"question" "q-124032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "04b92a1cec458cc88a24ef43c1fd8ceda302401a697fde2f9f475d70d295f959"} {"question" "q-124032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068004/state.json" "file_sha256" "25fe0d749d98b86c525e846a0064b3a8daa30c9fbd146fe466a73cd87ff66